      {
        "id": "p9-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the second derivative of $e^{2x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4e^{2x}$",
          "worked": "Let $f(x)=e^{2x}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} f'(x)&=2e^{2x}\\\\ f''(x)&=2(2e^{2x})\\\\ f''(x)&=4e^{2x}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that the $n$th derivative of $e^{2x}$ is $2^n e^{2x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{(n)}(x)=2^n e^{2x}$.\n\nEach differentiation supplies one factor of $2$.",
          "worked": "The first and second derivatives are $2e^{2x}$ and $2^2e^{2x}$. If the $m$th derivative is $2^m e^{2x}$, then\n\n$$\\begin{align*} f^{(m+1)}(x)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\bigl(2^m e^{2x}\\bigr)\\\\ f^{(m+1)}(x)&=2^m\\cdot 2e^{2x}\\\\ f^{(m+1)}(x)&=2^{m+1}e^{2x}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe first derivative starts the pattern, and the same step extends it to every positive integer $n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      }
    ]
  },
